      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-240518",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/64/641b6bc608c98f5855f89ecdd248e3a265fd1e29ede74e21c1d26e5731e1b2e8.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $CD^(\u27f7)$ is the perpendicular bisector of $AB^(\u2015)$.\n\nConsider the following statement:\n\n> $DB = DA$\n\nThe table below gives the proof of this statement. Select the correct options in the table and thus complete the proof.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $m\u2220DCB = 90^{\u2218}$ | Definition of perpendicular lines |\n| 2 | $m\u2220DCA = 90^{\u2218}$ | Definition of perpendicular lines |\n| 3 | $AC^(\u2015)\u2245CB^(\u2015)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| 4 | $AC = CB$ | Definition of congruent segments |\n| 5 | $DA^{2} = AC^{2} + DC^{2}$ | Pythagorean theorem for $\u25b3DCA$ |\n| 6 | $DA^{2} = CB^{2} + DC^{2}$ | {{field-2}} |\n| 7 | $CB^{2} + DC^{2} = DA^{2}$ | Symmetric property of equality |\n| 8 | $DB^{2} = CB^{2} + DC^{2}$ | Pythagorean theorem for $\u25b3DCB$ |\n| 9 | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |\n| 10 | $DB = DA$ | Algebra |"
    }
